From 187029b409d9feda6ce1935a7d3a09631af38862 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tstoltz <tristan.stoltz@evolvingresonantcocreationism.com>
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 2026 12:17:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add Model O (external verifier returns O; Nix owns native
 policy)

Fifth model in the admission-boundary architecture comparison for
nix-signature-policy (see /srv/luminous-dynamics/nix-signature-policy),
built on its own branch from the shared foundation (11a967a), not on
top of Model H's commits -- per this comparison's isolation discipline,
so O-process's own measurements aren't contaminated by H's cumulative
diff.

The provider is handed *raw, unverified* evidence (the canonical
fingerprint and literal `Sig:` entries, verbatim) and returns
*observations* it positively verified -- never a final decision. Nix
evaluates its own native grouped-signature policy
(allSignatureGroupsSatisfied, cherry-picked from Model N (8ad4bbe) --
the two models deliberately share this policy vocabulary, since Model
O's whole premise is delegating evidence verification, not policy) over
whatever the provider reports, composed with the ordinary
trusted-public-keys check.

New settings, distinctly named from Model H's (signature-observation-
provider vs signature-authorization-hook) so the two models'
configuration surfaces stay comparably measurable: signature-
observation-provider, -mode (legacy|conjunctive), -timeout-ms, and
signature-key-group (repeatable <group>:<key>[,<key>...] entries).

New src/libutil/signature-observation-caller.{hh,cc}: recreated (not
copied) from Model H's hardened process-invocation pattern -- same
safety properties (direct execve, no shell/PATH lookup, cleared
environment, reader threads armed before stdin write, dual process-
group/direct-pid SIGKILL on timeout, failed-invocation output never
inspected) -- but a different request/response shape throughout (raw
evidence out, observations in, never a decision), so O's diff doesn't
carry H-specific dead code it never calls.

Real end-to-end verification, not just compiled -- reused
nix-signature-policy's new nix-signature-verify-raw binary (R->O only,
committed there this same session) as the actual external provider via
a thin registry-merging wrapper script:

  - Full evidence (both frozen publisher keys), conjunctive mode: admits.
  - Incomplete evidence (provider's own registry knows only the Ed25519
    half), conjunctive mode: refuses -- the group requirement correctly
    isn't satisfied by partial evidence.
  - Legacy mode with a provider configured: admits, provider never
    invoked (confirmed via a sentinel file the provider would create if
    launched).
  - Crashing provider, conjunctive mode: fails closed.

Found and fixed one real bug while building this: the caller's
deliberately cleared child environment (no PATH, matching Model H's
same hardening) broke the first wrapper-script draft, which called
`python3` by bare name -- the shell inside the child process couldn't
resolve it. Fixed by using absolute paths throughout the wrapper, a
concrete illustration of exactly the operational cost "cleared
environment" carries for anyone deploying a provider that isn't a
single static binary.

Also independently rediscovered, before checking prior notes, the same
per-store-setting invocation-mechanism bug already documented in
H_INDEPENDENT_VERIFICATION.md (`--option` and NIX_CONFIG both produce
"unknown setting" warnings and silently no-op; only store-URI query
parameters work) -- and found the further wrinkle that query-string
values containing `:`/`,` need percent-encoding
(signature-key-group's `<group>:<key>,<key>` shape hits this directly).

Verified: nix-store-tests 617/617 (Model N's tests included via cherry-
pick; Model T's are not, since Model T isn't on this branch).
